# Which one is Matthew? Caravaggio's Calling of Saint Matthew
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}
prompt: "Where does the light point, and where does Christ's hand point? Is Matthew the bearded man or the young man counting coins?"
next: {"title":"Who is the painter painting? Velázquez's Las Meninas","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/the-painting-knows-youre-there/05-who-is-the-painter-painting-velazquez-s.cast"}

## The Calling of Saint Matthew: which one is he?
sources:
    - id: wiki
      title: The Calling of Saint Matthew
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: '1599–1600, Contarelli Chapel; Matthew 9:9; most identify Matthew as the bearded man, supported by the same model in the chapel''s two other Matthew paintings; an alternative reading has him pointing at the young man counting coins; Pope Francis: ''This is me, a sinner on whom the Lord has turned his gaze'''
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Calling_of_Saint_Matthew
    - id: chapel
      title: Contarelli Chapel
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Caravaggio's first major public commission (contract July 1599); the side paintings are lit by a beam from the direction of the chapel's only natural light; the table group wears dress of Caravaggio's time
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contarelli_Chapel
    - id: spadaro
      title: 'A Big Heart Open to God: an interview with Pope Francis'
      authors: Antonio Spadaro
      year: 2013
      finding: Francis often visited San Luigi dei Francesi to contemplate the Calling; 'This is me, a sinner on whom the Lord has turned his gaze'

    image art cites ["wiki","chapel"] credit "Caravaggio, 1599–1600. Contarelli Chapel, San Luigi dei Francesi, Rome. Public domain." x 500 y 345 width 660 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Michelangelo_Caravaggio_040.jpg/1920px-Michelangelo_Caravaggio_040.jpg look screen regions.back_youth [0.43,0.46,0.22,0.48] regions.beam [0.6,0.27,0.4,0.14] regions.beam_band [0.58,0.28,0.42,0.1] regions.beam_entry [0.82,0.1,0.18,0.24] regions.bearded [0.276,0.41,0.104,0.16] regions.bearded_finger [0.3,0.525,0.08,0.045] regions.both_candidates [0.18,0.4,0.2,0.27] regions.coin_counter [0.19,0.54,0.09,0.13] regions.coins [0.25,0.645,0.05,0.03] regions.gap [0.5,0.37,0.13,0.08] regions.hats [0.4,0.4,0.27,0.15] regions.jesus_arm [0.64,0.33,0.36,0.14] regions.jesus_face [0.845,0.35,0.065,0.07] regions.jesus_hand [0.615,0.385,0.06,0.045] regions.jesus_peter [0.64,0.33,0.36,0.64] regions.lit_faces [0.27,0.4,0.25,0.18] regions.peter_hand [0.728,0.5,0.065,0.05] regions.table_crowd [0.15,0.37,0.53,0.31] regions.table_men [0.06,0.4,0.6,0.55] hidden true
    portrait caravaggio x 910 y 470 width 160 of Caravaggio cameo true hidden true
    image insp cites ["wiki"] credit "Caravaggio, The Inspiration of Saint Matthew, 1602. Public domain." x 300 y 535 width 150 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f8/The_Inspiration_of_Saint_Matthew_by_Caravaggio.jpg look screen reveal develop hidden true
    arrow beam_arrow #f2c14e from.x 863 from.y 498 to.x 738 to.y 461 hidden true

    card title "The Calling of Saint Matthew"

Caravaggio painted this for a chapel in Rome in 1600.
    draw art

Its title promise (@highlight art:table_men strength soft@)s Saint Matthew — but which one is he?

The story is from the Gospel: Jesus passes a tax collector named Ma (@highlight art:table_men lift true@)tthew at his money table and says, follow me.

Caravaggio, not yet thirty, set it in a room of his own Rome, in his first big church commission.
    draw caravaggio

    erase caravaggio

Jesus and Pete (@highlight art:jesus_peter lift true@)r come in from the Bible, barefoot and in plain old robes.

The men at the table wear the fe (@highlight art:hats lift true@)athered hats and swords of Caravaggio's own day.

    camera on ["art:jesus_hand"] zoom 3

Jesus says nothing we can (@highlight art:jesus_hand strength strong lift true@) hear. He points.

Above his hand, a beam of li (@highlight art:beam_band strength strong lift true@)ght crosses the wall, travelling the same way, towards the table.

    camera reset true

It comes in fr (@draw beam_arrow@)om beyond the picture's right edge, not from the window painted on the wall.

    erase beam_arrow

    ask "One more hand copies Jesus's gesture. Tap it." spot peter_hand on art
        intro: The hand and the beam are not the only things pointing.
        right: Peter's, just below: a smaller echo of the call.
        wrong: Look just below Jesus's arm, in the shadow.

So the hand, its ech (@highlight art:jesus_hand art:peter_hand art:lit_faces lift true@)o and the beam all run to the same few faces at the table.

    camera on ["art@[0.08,0.36,0.56,0.36]"]

And at the table, a bearde (@point at.ref art:bearded_finger gesture glow@)d man answers with a finger of his own.

Most read it as pointing at himself — me? — and most take him for Matthew.
    highlight art:bearded

    camera reset true

Their strongest reason hangs in the same chapel: in Ca (@draw insp@)ravaggio's two other paintings there, Matthew is a bearded man too.

    erase insp

    camera on ["art@[0.08,0.36,0.56,0.36]"]

But the finger could just as well aim past his (@highlight art:coin_counter strength strong rest grey lift true@) chest, at the young man at the far left end of the table, still bent over the coins.

Some art historians argue that he is Matthew, and the bearded man is asking: him? The one the call hasn't reached yet.
    highlight art:coin_counter lift true

The gesture fits both, Caravaggio left no note, and the argument is still going.
    highlight art:both_candidates lift true

    camera reset true

Now find yourself. The young man on th (@highlight art:back_youth lift true@)e stool has his back to us, so we stand just behind him, on the open side of the table.

And the painted beam falls from the side of the chapel's real window, as if the daylight around us were the light in their room.
    highlight art:beam_band lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.08,0.3,0.7,0.42]"]

Every pointer in the picture runs into that crowd at the table, and the last one stops short.
    highlight art:table_crowd rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

One viewer took that personally: Pope Francis used to come and look at it whenever he stayed in Rome.
    highlight art:table_men strength soft lift true

Looking at the man b (@highlight art:table_men lift true@)eing called, he said: This is me, a sinner on whom the Lord has turned his gaze.

    quiz "A poster wants every passer-by to wonder: does this mean me? Which design follows Caravaggio?"
        right: The finger into the crowd: like Caravaggio's, it stops short, so each viewer finishes it, and some finish it at themselves.
        wrong: In the painting, does anyone point straight at you, or does the pointing stop short?
        * A finger aimed straight out at you
        * A spotlight on one named man
        + A finger aimed into a crowd, picking no one clearly
        * No gesture, just the words

    camera on ["art@[0.25,0.3,0.5,0.25]"]

Which one is Matthew? Caravaggio's finger stops short, and leaves the last few inches to you.
    highlight art:gap strength strong lift true

